=== layout: one-panel

# Biochemistry Lecture

@body

- BCHM 355/455, [Spring 2027]{color=blue}
- Thursday afternoons, 1:30-4:30 p.m.
- Face-to-face, Schaumburg
- We go into great detail on protein structure, enzyme function, and other macromolecules.
- Protein purification, separation methods, and enzyme kinetics.
- Protein-structure assignments: make images in UCSF Chimera and explain what they show.
- Connect a molecule's structure with what it does.

=== layout: one-panel

# Biochemistry Lab

@body

- BCHM 356/456, [Spring 2027]{color=blue}
- Face-to-face, Schaumburg
- Less structured and more independent study-like
- Enzyme assays and kinetics: measure reaction rates and interpret the data.
- Isolation and analysis of biological macromolecules.
- Explore protein sequence, structure, and folding using molecular software and databases.
- Experimental design, data collection, graphs, and interpretation are part of the lab work.

=== layout: one-panel

# Cellular and Molecular Biology

@body

- BIOL 301, [Spring 2027]{color=blue}
- The chemistry of life: biological molecules and how they work in cells.
- Cell structure, membranes, transport, and communication.
- Cellular energy: respiration and photosynthesis.
- DNA, gene expression, and regulation.
- The course combines lecture, laboratory, and discussion.
- [My teaching assignment is not confirmed yet.]{color=red}

=== layout: one-panel

# Biology and Ethics in Film

@body

- BIOL 383/483, [Summer 2027]{color=purple}
- Tuesday/Thursday evenings, 6-9 p.m., over Zoom
- Watch a feature film, then discuss its biology, science, and ethical questions.
- Weekly journal prompts ask you to make a case, include your opinions, and think critically.
- Most of the grade is a weekly writing prompt; getting it done is key.
- Give quick post-film "hot takes" and make a group movie-review podcast.
